  "content": {
    "title": "Chipotle offering BOGO deal for customers ‘tatted like a Chipotle bag’",
    "text": "FILE – The Chipotle Mexican Grill logo is seen on a storefront, Oct. 14, 2022, in Boston. (AP Photo/Michael Dwyer, File) (NewsNation) — Chipotle is reviving its “tatted like a Chipotle bag” buy-one-get-one free deal this Friday. On March 13, from 3 to 4 p.m. local time, customers with tattoos will receive a free entrée with the purchase of another one. “A tattoo can mean a real tattoo, a temporary tattoo, a drawn-on tattoo, or another creative iteration invoking tattoos,” the company stated in a release. The term “tatted like a Chipotle bag” was inspired by Maroon 5 singer Adam Levine’s shirtless look at the 2019 NFL Super Bowl, when his “tattoos were compared on social media to the artwork on Chipotle’s to-go bags,” according to the release. Adam Levine of Maroon 5 performs during halftime of the NFL Super Bowl 53 football game between the Los Angeles Rams and the New England Patriots Sunday, Feb. 3, 2019, in Atlanta. (AP Photo/Mark Humphrey)Adam Levine of Maroon 5 performs during halftime of the NFL Super Bowl 53 football game between the Los Angeles Rams and the New England Patriots Sunday, Feb. 3, 2019, in Atlanta. (AP Photo/Mark Humphrey) Chipotle offered the promotion for the first time on Friday, June 13, 2025, which it says drove the company’s “highest-ever sales during the 3 to 4 p.m. non-peak hour.” “What started as an internet meme became a real-world expression of fandom for our brand,” Stephanie Perdue, interim chief marketing officer, said in the release. This time around, Chipotle is tapping tatted hip-hop superstar Swae Lee to join the collab and announced a temporary tattoo flash sheet co-created by the artist available exclusively at the Chipotle in Midtown Miami on Friday. On Wednesday, the company released an advertisement on Instagram for the upcoming promotion featuring Lee’s song “Black Beatles,” which spurred the viral “mannequin challenge” on social media in 2016. NAVI MUMBAI, INDIA – NOVEMBER 22: Swae Lee performs on stage at Loud Park on November 22, 2025 in Navi Mumbai, India. (Photo by Matt Jelonek/Getty Images) “Gen Z is obsessed with all things nostalgia, driving a flurry of ‘2026 is the new 2016’ social media content since January to reminisce about their favorite viral moments from 10 years ago,” the release states.",
